{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E6YDAajN9rQ1Ex1NzUHjvN","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# ESLint Rule Patterns Reference\n     2→\n     3→This reference covers the mechanics of writing and wiring ESLint rules. It tells you\n     4→HOW to create rules — WHAT to enforce is determined by the codebase context.\n     5→\n     6→## Flat Config: Adding Custom Rules\n     7→\n     8→### Inline Plugin (no npm package needed)\n     9→\n    10→```js\n    11→// eslint.config.js\n    12→import myRule from './eslint-rules/my-rule.js';\n    13→\n    14→const driftGuard = {\n    15→  rules: {\n    16→    'my-rule': myRule,\n    17→    // add more rules here\n    18→  }\n    19→};\n    20→\n    21→export default [\n    22→  // ...other config\n    23→  {\n    24→    plugins: { 'drift-guard': driftGuard },\n    25→    rules: {\n    26→      'drift-guard/my-rule': 'warn',\n    27→    }\n    28→  }\n    29→];\n    30→```\n    31→\n    32→### Directory-scoped overrides\n    33→\n    34→Different directories may need different rules. For instance, an abstraction layer\n    35→should be allowed to use the low-level API it wraps:\n    36→\n    37→```js\n    38→export default [\n    39→  {\n    40→    files: ['src/**/*.ts', 'src/**/*.tsx'],\n    41→    plugins: { 'drift-guard': driftGuard },\n    42→    rules: {\n    43→      'drift-guard/my-rule': 'warn',\n    44→    }\n    45→  },\n    46→  {\n    47→    // Override: allow the abstraction layer itself to use the low-level API\n    48→    files: ['src/some-layer/**/*.ts'],\n    49→    rules: {\n    50→      'drift-guard/my-rule': 'off',\n    51→    }\n    52→  }\n    53→];\n    54→```\n    55→\n    56→---\n    57→\n    58→## Rule Mechanisms\n    59→\n    60→### 1. no-restricted-imports\n    61→\n    62→**Use for:** Banning specific import paths or patterns.\n    63→\n    64→```js\n    65→rules: {\n    66→  'no-restricted-imports': ['warn', {\n    67→    // Ban specific paths\n    68→    paths: [\n    69→      {\n    70→        name: 'some-module',\n    71→        message: 'Use X instead. See docs/patterns/whatever.md'\n    72→      },\n    73→      {\n    74→        name: 'some-module',\n    75→        importNames: ['specificExport'],\n    76→        message: 'specificExport is deprecated. Use Y instead.'\n    77→      }\n    78→    ],\n    79→    // Ban path patterns\n    80→    patterns: [\n    81→      {\n    82→        group: ['**/internal/*', '!**/internal/index'],\n    83→        message: 'Import from the public API (index) instead of internal modules.'\n    84→      }\n    85→    ]\n    86→  }]\n    87→}\n    88→```\n    89→\n    90→### 2. no-restricted-syntax\n    91→\n    92→**Use for:** Banning specific code patterns using AST selectors.\n    93→\n    94→AST selectors use the same syntax as CSS selectors but for code. Use\n    95→[AST Explorer](https://astexplorer.net) to find the right selector.\n    96→\n    97→```js\n    98→rules: {\n    99→  'no-restricted-syntax': ['warn',\n   100→    {\n   101→      // Ban a specific function call pattern\n   102→      selector: \"CallExpression[callee.name='dangerousFunction']\",\n   103→      message: 'Use safeAlternative() instead.'\n   104→    },\n   105→    {\n   106→      // Ban a member expression pattern (e.g., obj.method())\n   107→      selector: \"CallExpression[callee.object.name='obj'][callee.property.name='method']\",\n   108→      message: 'Use the wrapper hook instead of calling obj.method() directly.'\n   109→    },\n   110→    {\n   111→      // Ban module-level let declarations\n   112→      selector: \"Program > VariableDeclaration[kind='let']\",\n   113→      message: 'Avoid module-level mutable state.'\n   114→    }\n   115→  ]\n   116→}\n   117→```\n   118→\n   119→**Common AST selector patterns:**\n   120→\n   121→| What you want to match | Selector |\n   122→|------------------------|----------|\n   123→| Function call `foo()` | `CallExpression[callee.name='foo']` |\n   124→| Method call `a.b()` | `CallExpression[callee.object.name='a'][callee.property.name='b']` |\n   125→| Chained call `a.b.c()` | `CallExpression[callee.object.type='MemberExpression'][callee.object.object.name='a']` |\n   126→| JSX element `<Foo>` | `JSXOpeningElement[name.name='Foo']` |\n   127→| JSX attribute `prop={val}` | `JSXAttribute[name.name='prop']` |\n   128→| Variable with kind | `VariableDeclaration[kind='let']` |\n   129→| Top-level only | `Program > [selector]` |\n   130→| Inside a function | `FunctionDeclaration [selector]` |\n   131→| Containing something | `ForStatement:has(CatchClause)` |\n   132→\n   133→### 3. Custom Rule Module\n   134→\n   135→**Use for:** Anything more complex — file-path-aware rules, counting/threshold rules,\n   136→cross-node analysis, auto-fixable rules.\n   137→\n   138→```js\n   139→// eslint-rules/example-rule.js\n   140→export default {\n   141→  meta: {\n   142→    type: 'suggestion',           // 'problem' | 'suggestion' | 'layout'\n   143→    docs: {\n   144→      description: 'What this rule does',\n   145→    },\n   146→    messages: {\n   147→      // Named messages with optional placeholders\n   148→      ruleViolation: 'Found {{thing}} — use {{alternative}} instead. See {{docLink}}',\n   149→    },\n   150→    // fixable: 'code',           // uncomment if rule provides auto-fixes\n   151→    schema: [],                    // options schema (empty = no options)\n   152→  },\n   153→\n   154→  create(context) {\n   155→    // context.filename — current file path (use for directory-aware rules)\n   156→    // context.report({ node, messageId, data }) — report a violation\n   157→    // context.getSourceCode() — access the full AST\n   158→\n   159→    return {\n   160→      // Visitor methods — named after AST node types\n   161→      // See https://astexplorer.net for node types\n   162→\n   163→      CallExpression(node) {\n   164→        // Your detection logic here\n   165→        if (/* violation detected */) {\n   166→          context.report({\n   167→            node,\n   168→            messageId: 'ruleViolation',\n   169→            data: {\n   170→              thing: 'what was found',\n   171→              alternative: 'what to use instead',\n   172→              docLink: 'docs/patterns/relevant.md',\n   173→            },\n   174→          });\n   175→        }\n   176→      },\n   177→\n   178→      ImportDeclaration(node) {\n   179→        // Check import source: node.source.value\n   180→        // Check imported names: node.specifiers\n   181→      },\n   182→\n   183→      JSXOpeningElement(node) {\n   184→        // Check component name: node.name.name (for simple names)\n   185→        // Check attributes: node.attributes\n   186→      },\n   187→    };\n   188→  },\n   189→};\n   190→```\n   191→\n   192→**File-path-aware rule example:**\n   193→```js\n   194→create(context) {\n   195→  const filename = context.filename || context.getFilename();\n   196→  const isInAllowedDirectory = /some-directory[/\\\\]/.test(filename);\n   197→  if (isInAllowedDirectory) return {};  // skip enforcement in this directory\n   198→\n   199→  return {\n   200→    // ... visitors\n   201→  };\n   202→}\n   203→```\n   204→\n   205→**Counting rule example (e.g., max N of something per file):**\n   206→```js\n   207→create(context) {\n   208→  let count = 0;\n   209→  const MAX = 10;\n   210→\n   211→  return {\n   212→    'SomeNode'() {\n   213→      count++;\n   214→    },\n   215→    'Program:exit'() {\n   216→      if (count > MAX) {\n   217→        context.report({\n   218→          node: context.getSourceCode().ast,\n   219→          message: `File has ${count} occurrences (max ${MAX}). Consider refactoring.`,\n   220→        });\n   221→      }\n   222→    },\n   223→  };\n   224→}\n   225→```\n   226→\n   227→---\n   228→\n   229→## Testing Rules\n   230→\n   231→```js\n   232→// eslint-rules/__tests__/example-rule.test.js\n   233→import { RuleTester } from 'eslint';\n   234→import rule from '../example-rule.js';\n   235→\n   236→const tester = new RuleTester({\n   237→  languageOptions: { ecmaVersion: 2022, sourceType: 'module' }\n   238→});\n   239→\n   240→tester.run('example-rule', rule, {\n   241→  valid: [\n   242→    // Code that should NOT trigger the rule\n   243→    { code: 'goodPattern()', filename: 'src/components/Foo.tsx' },\n   244→    // Code in an exempt directory\n   245→    { code: 'exemptedCall()', filename: 'src/allowed-dir/Bar.ts' },\n   246→  ],\n   247→  invalid: [\n   248→    // Code that SHOULD trigger the rule\n   249→    {\n   250→      code: 'badPattern()',\n   251→      filename: 'src/components/Baz.tsx',\n   252→      errors: [{ messageId: 'ruleViolation' }],\n   253→    },\n   254→  ],\n   255→});\n   256→```\n   257→\n   258→---\n   259→\n   260→## Severity Strategy\n   261→\n   262→| Phase | Severity | Purpose |\n   263→|-------|----------|---------|\n   264→| Initial rollout | `warn` | Surface violations without blocking |\n   265→| After cleanup | `error` | Hard gate, fails CI |\n   266→| Intentional exceptions | `// eslint-disable-next-line drift-guard/rule -- reason` | Documented bypass |\n   267→\n   268→Consider requiring a reason for all disable comments using `eslint-comments/require-description`\n   269→if the project uses `eslint-plugin-eslint-comments`.\n   270→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13268b0-3558-4a8f-abab-805b852d788c","uuid":"d41dbf4c-4603-4bb9-93ff-1a5eb424e18e","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/tsonu/.drift-semantic/skill/drift-guard/references/eslint-rule-patterns.md","content":"# ESLint Rule Patterns Reference\n\nThis reference covers the mechanics of writing and wiring ESLint rules. It tells you\nHOW to create rules — WHAT to enforce is determined by the codebase context.\n\n## Flat Config: Adding Custom Rules\n\n### Inline Plugin (no npm package needed)\n\n```js\n// eslint.config.js\nimport myRule from './eslint-rules/my-rule.js';\n\nconst driftGuard = {\n  rules: {\n    'my-rule': myRule,\n    // add more rules here\n  }\n};\n\nexport default [\n  // ...other config\n  {\n    plugins: { 'drift-guard': driftGuard },\n    rules: {\n      'drift-guard/my-rule': 'warn',\n    }\n  }\n];\n```\n\n### Directory-scoped overrides\n\nDifferent directories may need different rules. For instance, an abstraction layer\nshould be allowed to use the low-level API it wraps:\n\n```js\nexport default [\n  {\n    files: ['src/**/*.ts', 'src/**/*.tsx'],\n    plugins: { 'drift-guard': driftGuard },\n    rules: {\n      'drift-guard/my-rule': 'warn',\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    // Override: allow the abstraction layer itself to use the low-level API\n    files: ['src/some-layer/**/*.ts'],\n    rules: {\n      'drift-guard/my-rule': 'off',\n    }\n  }\n];\n```\n\n---\n\n## Rule Mechanisms\n\n### 1. no-restricted-imports\n\n**Use for:** Banning specific import paths or patterns.\n\n```js\nrules: {\n  'no-restricted-imports': ['warn', {\n    // Ban specific paths\n    paths: [\n      {\n        name: 'some-module',\n        message: 'Use X instead. See docs/patterns/whatever.md'\n      },\n      {\n        name: 'some-module',\n        importNames: ['specificExport'],\n        message: 'specificExport is deprecated. Use Y instead.'\n      }\n    ],\n    // Ban path patterns\n    patterns: [\n      {\n        group: ['**/internal/*', '!**/internal/index'],\n        message: 'Import from the public API (index) instead of internal modules.'\n      }\n    ]\n  }]\n}\n```\n\n### 2. no-restricted-syntax\n\n**Use for:** Banning specific code patterns using AST selectors.\n\nAST selectors use the same syntax as CSS selectors but for code. Use\n[AST Explorer](https://astexplorer.net) to find the right selector.\n\n```js\nrules: {\n  'no-restricted-syntax': ['warn',\n    {\n      // Ban a specific function call pattern\n      selector: \"CallExpression[callee.name='dangerousFunction']\",\n      message: 'Use safeAlternative() instead.'\n    },\n    {\n      // Ban a member expression pattern (e.g., obj.method())\n      selector: \"CallExpression[callee.object.name='obj'][callee.property.name='method']\",\n      message: 'Use the wrapper hook instead of calling obj.method() directly.'\n    },\n    {\n      // Ban module-level let declarations\n      selector: \"Program > VariableDeclaration[kind='let']\",\n      message: 'Avoid module-level mutable state.'\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Common AST selector patterns:**\n\n| What you want to match | Selector |\n|------------------------|----------|\n| Function call `foo()` | `CallExpression[callee.name='foo']` |\n| Method call `a.b()` | `CallExpression[callee.object.name='a'][callee.property.name='b']` |\n| Chained call `a.b.c()` | `CallExpression[callee.object.type='MemberExpression'][callee.object.object.name='a']` |\n| JSX element `<Foo>` | `JSXOpeningElement[name.name='Foo']` |\n| JSX attribute `prop={val}` | `JSXAttribute[name.name='prop']` |\n| Variable with kind | `VariableDeclaration[kind='let']` |\n| Top-level only | `Program > [selector]` |\n| Inside a function | `FunctionDeclaration [selector]` |\n| Containing something | `ForStatement:has(CatchClause)` |\n\n### 3. Custom Rule Module\n\n**Use for:** Anything more complex — file-path-aware rules, counting/threshold rules,\ncross-node analysis, auto-fixable rules.\n\n```js\n// eslint-rules/example-rule.js\nexport default {\n  meta: {\n    type: 'suggestion',           // 'problem' | 'suggestion' | 'layout'\n    docs: {\n      description: 'What this rule does',\n    },\n    messages: {\n      // Named messages with optional placeholders\n      ruleViolation: 'Found {{thing}} — use {{alternative}} instead. See {{docLink}}',\n    },\n    // fixable: 'code',           // uncomment if rule provides auto-fixes\n    schema: [],                    // options schema (empty = no options)\n  },\n\n  create(context) {\n    // context.filename — current file path (use for directory-aware rules)\n    // context.report({ node, messageId, data }) — report a violation\n    // context.getSourceCode() — access the full AST\n\n    return {\n      // Visitor methods — named after AST node types\n      // See https://astexplorer.net for node types\n\n      CallExpression(node) {\n        // Your detection logic here\n        if (/* violation detected */) {\n          context.report({\n            node,\n            messageId: 'ruleViolation',\n            data: {\n              thing: 'what was found',\n              alternative: 'what to use instead',\n              docLink: 'docs/patterns/relevant.md',\n            },\n          });\n        }\n      },\n\n      ImportDeclaration(node) {\n        // Check import source: node.source.value\n        // Check imported names: node.specifiers\n      },\n\n      JSXOpeningElement(node) {\n        // Check component name: node.name.name (for simple names)\n        // Check attributes: node.attributes\n      },\n    };\n  },\n};\n```\n\n**File-path-aware rule example:**\n```js\ncreate(context) {\n  const filename = context.filename || context.getFilename();\n  const isInAllowedDirectory = /some-directory[/\\\\]/.test(filename);\n  if (isInAllowedDirectory) return {};  // skip enforcement in this directory\n\n  return {\n    // ... visitors\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**Counting rule example (e.g., max N of something per file):**\n```js\ncreate(context) {\n  let count = 0;\n  const MAX = 10;\n\n  return {\n    'SomeNode'() {\n      count++;\n    },\n    'Program:exit'() {\n      if (count > MAX) {\n        context.report({\n          node: context.getSourceCode().ast,\n          message: `File has ${count} occurrences (max ${MAX}). Consider refactoring.`,\n        });\n      }\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Rules\n\n```js\n// eslint-rules/__tests__/example-rule.test.js\nimport { RuleTester } from 'eslint';\nimport rule from '../example-rule.js';\n\nconst tester = new RuleTester({\n  languageOptions: { ecmaVersion: 2022, sourceType: 'module' }\n});\n\ntester.run('example-rule', rule, {\n  valid: [\n    // Code that should NOT trigger the rule\n    { code: 'goodPattern()', filename: 'src/components/Foo.tsx' },\n    // Code in an exempt directory\n    { code: 'exemptedCall()', filename: 'src/allowed-dir/Bar.ts' },\n  ],\n  invalid: [\n    // Code that SHOULD trigger the rule\n    {\n      code: 'badPattern()',\n      filename: 'src/components/Baz.tsx',\n      errors: [{ messageId: 'ruleViolation' }],\n    },\n  ],\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n## Severity Strategy\n\n| Phase | Severity | Purpose |\n|-------|----------|---------|\n| Initial rollout | `warn` | Surface violations without blocking |\n| After cleanup | `error` | Hard gate, fails CI |\n| Intentional exceptions | `// eslint-disable-next-line drift-guard/rule -- reason` | Documented bypass |\n\nConsider requiring a reason for all disable comments using `eslint-comments/require-description`\nif the project uses `eslint-plugin-eslint-comments`.\n","numLines":270,"startLine":1,"totalLines":270}}}
