{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JnY2eN4Bwzgno8e7WGXYdr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key finding: The `@the-canonry/world-store` package's `main` and `exports` already point to `./src/index.ts` — the source TypeScript file! This means that even without the Vite alias, the package resolution will point to the source file. The alias is redundant because the package.json already exports the source directly.\n\nSo for archivist and chronicler, removing the `@the-canonry/world-store` alias is safe — the `workspace:*` resolution through pnpm will resolve to the package root, and the package's `exports` field maps `.` to `./src/index.ts`, which is exactly the same file the alias was pointing to.\n \nNow looking at viewer — it's importing chronicler's source files directly like `@chronicler/ChroniclerRemote.tsx` and assets, which creates a cross-app source coupling. Viewer doesn't use module federation at all; it's just a standalone app with the React plugin that bundles chronicler's code directly into itself.\n\nThe real question is whether viewer should be using module federation to consume chronicler as a remote, or if it's legitimately a wrapper app where direct imports are acceptable. Given that it only has two source imports from chronicler, this might be a justified exception for a deployment wrapper, but I should verify what viewer actually does before deciding.\n\nNow checking cosmographer's usage of the `@name-forge` alias.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1437,"cache_read_input_tokens":58408,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1437},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"723edc89-4635-4bba-9365-c75d4fe3bc31","uuid":"36be98c4-1dfa-4263-89a0-ab03d645d734"}
