{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NQaS2D3F8zpXbHgEw2ZSKY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The existing ESLint rule `no-duplicate-component-css` is already wired into the config and would catch re-addition of `dropdown.css` to coherence-engine's `styles/components/` directory. It's already at `warn` severity.\n\nThe existing ADR-023 covers exactly this pattern. The existing pattern doc covers the pattern.\n\nI need to:\n1. Update ADR-023 to mention css-cluster-006 (dropdown.css) as an additional area it covers\n2. Update the pattern doc to include dropdown as an example\n3. Since the ESLint rule already exists and is wired, I don't need a NEW rule — but I should verify it would catch the drift\n\n\n4. I'm reconsidering whether I need a separate ADR for this specific area or if updating ADR-023 is sufficient, since the underlying principle is the same — just applied to another file. The instructions require machine-enforceable rules for every area, so I need to confirm the existing rule covers this case before deciding whether to consolidate or create a new ADR.\n\nNow I'm realizing the `no-duplicate-component-css` rule already handles this through its import scanning mechanism — it resolves CSS imports and checks for duplicate component files, so it catches the duplication even though it can't directly lint CSS files themselves. The ESLint rule handles accidental recreation of the deleted file, but someone could work around it by creating a different filename with the same styles — that's something only code review can catch. I'll add the dropdown overrides as an example in the pattern documentation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5053,"cache_read_input_tokens":80153,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5053},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo"