{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015ezpKaUnYhnQkwDTQ6SXZ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to create an ESLint rule that prevents the old per-component CSS class patterns from being reintroduced. The drift was: each view component had its own prefixed CSS classes for the same overlay panel pattern (e.g., `gv-legend`, `gv3d-legend`, `tv3d-legend`). After unification, the canonical approach uses shared `viz-*` classes from `visualization-overlay.css`.\n\nWhat can I enforce with an ESLint rule?\n\n1. **Ban old CSS class names in JSX** — detect usage of the old prefixed class names in `className` attributes within the archivist app's visualization components. This would catch someone adding back `gv3d-legend` or `tv3d-container` instead of using `viz-legend` or `viz-container`.\n\n\n2. **Ban old CSS variable names** — detect `--gv-swatch-color`, `--gv3d-swatch-color`, `--tv3d-swatch-color` in style attributes.\n\nI'm thinking a custom rule that scans string literals in `className` attributes for the banned prefixes would work best, though `no-restricted-syntax` with a pattern could also work if I can match string content within className attributes. Let me check how the existing custom rules like `no-duplicate-component-css.js` handle this pattern.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2764,"cache_read_input_tokens":84512,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2764},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b63bbfa3-8e9b-4041-b254-e9bba5dedd64","uuid":"1c796c94-f7b4-44bf-a050-5af03e917cb0"}
