{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BTpo7hCxSYKJkYrzWokSUJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. The shared-components already has:\n- `empty-state.css` - with shared empty state classes\n- `section.css` - with section container/header classes\n- `card.css` - with card base classes\n- `button.css` - with button base classes\n- `utilities.css` - with utility classes (flex, gap, text, spacing)\n\nBUT the Illuminator components don't USE these shared styles. Each component re-invents these patterns with its own prefixed classes. The problem is that the shared-components use custom properties (like `--font-size-md`, `--color-text-muted`) while the Illuminator components use different custom properties (like `var(--text-muted)`, `var(--bg-tertiary)`, `var(--border-color)`).\n \nLooking at the theme.css to understand how the variables map. The 44% similarity across the 20 component CSS files actually comes from repeated micro-patterns—the same status colors showing up everywhere (`#10b981` for success, `#f59e0b` for warning, `#ef4444` for error) and the same section-card structure with secondary backgrounds and borders appearing across multiple components. I'm noticing the core issue—Illuminator uses its own design token naming convention (`--text-muted`, `--bg-secondary`) that differs from the shared-components package (`--color-text-muted`, `--color-bg-card`), so I need to create an Illuminator-specific utilities file that maps these tokens to reusable classes rather than trying to force consolidation into the existing shared package. I'm identifying the most frequently duplicated patterns across those 20 files to extract into the shared utilities file—things like the section card container with padding and background styling, the borderless variant, and the section header styles that appear repeatedly. These are the high-impact candidates that will reduce the most dupl