 question is what specific patterns or rules are actually duplicated versus just using the same tokens.\n\nNow I'm identifying the concrete duplicated CSS patterns that could be consolidated. The empty state styling appears across multiple files with nearly identical padding, centered text alignment, and muted color usage. Section labels follow a consistent pattern with small uppercase text, muted color, and letter spacing. Thumbnail images all use the same object-fit cover approach with full width and height. Selection bars repeat the same flexbox layout with space-between alignment and consistent padding.\n\nContinuing with the remaining patterns...\n\nSmall badges share consistent sizing with tiny font sizes and minimal padding, while stat cards and grids follow a predictable structure using CSS grid with auto-fit columns and consistent gap spacing.\n\nNow I'm realizing the core issue: these components aren't actually duplicating the same CSS classes—they're just following similar structural patterns. The 43% similarity is more about design consistency than actual code duplication. The practical solution is to extract reusable utility classes into the shared-components CSS file rather than trying to consolidate class definitions that don't overlap. I'm realizing 43% similarity isn't actually that high—these are genuinely different components, not duplicates. But since I need to make changes, the practical path is to extract the common micro-patterns like empty states and section labels into a shared utility CSS file, import it across all 17 components, and update their JSX to reference those shared classes while keeping component-specific styles intact. Let me check what utility CSS files already exist to avoid creating redundant ones.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21640,"cache_read_input_tokens":81523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":216