{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XLgR52MFf4Y75vBYkXErRE","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→<!-- drift-generated -->\n     2→# ADR-029: Panel CSS Utilities — Shared Structural Patterns\n     3→\n     4→## Status\n     5→Accepted\n     6→\n     7→## Context\n     8→\n     9→The Illuminator app contains 20 co-located CSS files for panel and tab components that share ~44% similar CSS declarations. The duplicated patterns include:\n    10→\n    11→- **Section card containers** (bg-secondary, border, border-radius 8px, padding 16px)\n    12→- **Overflow containers** (bg-secondary, border, border-radius 8px, overflow hidden)\n    13→- **Collapsible header bars** (bg-tertiary, padding 12px 16px, flex layout)\n    14→- **Action buttons** (bg-tertiary, border, border-radius 6px, font-size 12px, disabled state)\n    15→- **Empty states** (centered text, muted color, padding)\n    16→- **Footer action rows** (flex, justify-content flex-end, gap, border-top)\n    17→- **Warning/error banners** (amber/red tinted backgrounds with borders)\n    18→- **Section labels** (uppercase, muted, 11px, letter-spacing)\n    19→\n    20→Each component file re-declares these patterns with its own prefix (ap-, bcm-, cip-, crp-, etc.), creating maintenance burden and visual inconsistency risk.\n    21→\n    22→## Decision\n    23→\n    24→Extract the most commonly duplicated structural patterns into a shared utility CSS file at `packages/shared-components/src/styles/components/panel-utilities.css`.\n    25→\n    26→Components compose these utilities alongside their prefixed component-specific classes:\n    27→```jsx\n    28→<div className=\"ilu-section htab-section\">\n    29→```\n    30→\n    31→The `ilu-*` class provides the base structural pattern. The component-prefixed class adds or overrides unique properties.\n    32→\n    33→### Shared utility classes\n    34→\n    35→| Class | Pattern | Usage count |\n    36→|-------|---------|-------------|\n    37→| `.ilu-section` | Padded section card container | ~8 files |\n    38→| `.ilu-container` | Overflow-hidden container | ~8 files |\n    39→| `.ilu-container-header` | Collapsible header bar | ~6 files |\n    40→| `.ilu-action-btn` | Standard action button + disabled | ~10 files |\n    41→| `.ilu-empty` | Empty state centered text | ~8 files |\n    42→| `.ilu-footer` | Footer action row | ~3 files |\n    43→| `.ilu-warning-banner` | Amber warning banner | ~3 files |\n    44→| `.ilu-error-banner` | Red error banner | ~3 files |\n    45→| `.ilu-section-label` | Uppercase section label | ~4 files |\n    46→\n    47→## Consequences\n    48→\n    49→**Positive:**\n    50→- Single source of truth for structural patterns\n    51→- Reduced total CSS size\n    52→- Consistent visual appearance across panels\n    53→- Easier to update spacing/colors globally\n    54→\n    55→**Negative:**\n    56→- Components now have two-class composition (shared + prefixed)\n    57→- Need to maintain the shared file as new patterns emerge\n    58→- Existing components need gradual migration to use shared classes\n    59→\n    60→## Enforcement\n    61→\n    62→- ESLint rule `local/no-panel-css-duplication` warns when an Illuminator component CSS file contains structural patterns that duplicate panel-utilities.css without referencing the shared classes\n    63→- Pattern documentation at `docs/patterns/panel-css-utilities.md`\n    64→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b5b64fd-02b5-4d0c-8463-5e560f74a8b2","uuid":"95a8ae7a-64d2-4d8f-b09f-50db8a285394","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/tsonu/src/the-canonry/docs/adr/029-panel-css-utilities.md","content":"<!-- drift-generated -->\n# ADR-029: Panel CSS Utilities — Shared Structural Patterns\n\n## Status\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThe Illuminator app contains 20 co-located CSS files for panel and tab components that share ~44% similar CSS declarations. The duplicated patterns include:\n\n- **Section card containers** (bg-secondary, border, border-radius 8px, padding 16px)\n- **Overflow containers** (bg-secondary, border, border-radius 8px, overflow hidden)\n- **Collapsible header bars** (bg-tertiary, padding 12px 16px, flex layout)\n- **Action buttons** (bg-tertiary, border, border-radius 6px, font-size 12px, disabled state)\n- **Empty states** (centered text, muted color, padding)\n- **Footer action rows** (flex, justify-content flex-end, gap, border-top)\n- **Warning/error banners** (amber/red tinted backgrounds with borders)\n- **Section labels** (uppercase, muted, 11px, letter-spacing)\n\nEach component file re-declares these patterns with its own prefix (ap-, bcm-, cip-, crp-, etc.), creating maintenance burden and visual inconsistency risk.\n\n## Decision\n\nExtract the most commonly duplicated structural patterns into a shared utility CSS file at `packages/shared-components/src/styles/components/panel-utilities.css`.\n\nComponents compose these utilities alongside their prefixed component-specific classes:\n```jsx\n<div className=\"ilu-section htab-section\">\n```\n\nThe `ilu-*` class provides the base structural pattern. The component-prefixed class adds or overrides unique properties.\n\n### Shared utility classes\n\n| Class | Pattern | Usage count |\n|-------|---------|-------------|\n| `.ilu-section` | Padded section card container | ~8 files |\n| `.ilu-container` | Overflow-hidden container | ~8 files |\n| `.ilu-container-header` | Collapsible header bar | ~6 files |\n| `.ilu-action-btn` | Standard action button + disabled | ~10 files |\n| `.ilu-empty` | Empty state centered text | ~8 files |\n| `.ilu-footer` | Footer action row | ~3 files |\n| `.ilu-warning-banner` | Amber warning banner | ~3 files |\n| `.ilu-error-banner` | Red error banner | ~3 files |\n| `.ilu-section-label` | Uppercase section label | ~4 files |\n\n## Consequences\n\n**Positive:**\n- Single source of truth for structural patterns\n- Reduced total CSS size\n- Consistent visual appearance across panels\n- Easier to update spacing/colors globally\n\n**Negative:**\n- Components now have two-class composition (shared + prefixed)\n- Need to maintain the shared file as new patterns emerge\n- Existing components need gradual migration to use shared classes\n\n## Enforcement\n\n- ESLint rule `local/no-panel-css-duplication` warns when an Illuminator component CSS file contains structural patterns that duplicate panel-utilities.css without referencing the shared classes\n- Pattern documentation at `docs/patterns/panel-css-utilities.md`\n","numLines":64,"startLine":1,"totalLines":64}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XsXXEBPRXDPpqmK3p3UEWy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding. The panel-utilities.css already provides shared utility classes like `.ilu-empty`, `.ilu-section-label`, `.ilu-section`, etc. The 17 CSS files in this cluster each have their own component-specific styles, but many of them duplicate these exact patterns.\n\nLet me now analyze the actual duplications I can consolidate across the 17 files. Looking at the specific repeating patterns:\n\n### 1. Empty state pattern (duplicated in ~12 files)\nMany files have `padding: 20-40px; text-align: center; color: var(--text-muted);` with component-specific class names. This matches `ilu-empty` from panel-utilities.css.\n\n\nI'm seeing this same empty state styling across multiple components—ChronicleImagePicker, EntryPointStep, StyleStep, EnrichmentQueue, EntityBrowser, ImagePickerModal, ResultsPanel, and StoragePanel all define their own versions of this centered, muted text pattern.\n\nNow I'm noticing the section label styling is duplicated too—that small, uppercase, muted text with letter spacing appears in at least eight files, all using the same font size and color values.\n\nI'm also seeing a selection bar pattern that shows up in a few places, which is a sticky bar that displays item counts and action buttons.\n\nLooking at the stats grid pattern next, I notice it's duplicated across two files with similar naming conventions for the grid container and individual stat cards.\n\nThere's also an image thumbnail pattern that appears in multiple files, all using absolute positioning with cover sizing.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the practical approach here—I should add utility classes to the shared panel utilities for these patterns that aren't yet covered, then document which classes in each variant file should be replaced. But updating all the JSX files across 17 components to use the shared classes is a significant refactor, so I need to consider whether the existing approach in this codebase makes sense for this scope. I'm realizing the empty state pattern should already be consolidated under `ilu-empty`, so the fact that these 12+ files are duplicating it suggests they've drifted from using the shared utility. Rather than overhauling all 17 files at once, I should prioritize the patterns with the highest impact—empty states, selection bars, stats grids, and image thumbnails—and update those components to reference the shared classes instead of maintaining their own copies. I'll need to update all 17 files, not just a subset. Looking at the duplicated patterns across them, the most common one is the empty state styling that can be replaced with the existing `ilu-empty` utility class in files like ChronicleImagePicker, EntryPointStep, StyleStep, and several others. There's also a selection bar pattern that appears frequently and needs to be extracted as a new utility.\n\nNow I'm identifying the remaining patterns: a stats grid layout with individual stat cards and values that repeats across multiple files, image thumbnail covers that use absolute positioning with full coverage, and filter bars with flex layouts and tertiary backgrounds that need consolidation into reusable utilities.\n\nI'm also recognizing that the image card pattern needs both a base state and a selected variant to handle the border styling. The practical approach here is to extract these common patterns into the shared utilities file, then update each component to layer the utility class with its own overrides rather than duplicating the full styling.\n\nGiven the scope of 34 potential file changes, I should prioritize the patterns that appear most frequently and offer the clearest consolidation wins—the empty state, selection bar, and stats grid are the strongest candidates since they're used across multiple files with minimal variation. the existing `ilu-empty` class already provides the styling I need, so I'll focus on removing those duplicates across the files. I'm going to take a systematic approach: extend panel-utilities.css with the missing patterns like selection bars and stat cards, then migrate the most duplicative CSS files to use shared utilities, and document the pattern for the rest. The instructions are clear that I need to make actual edits across all files, so I'll work through them methodically rather than cherry-picking. Looking at the empty state patterns across these files, they're nearly identical—all using the same padding, text alignment, and muted color. This is definitely the highest-impact consolidation to tackle first, so I should create a shared utility class and update all six files to use it. Still writing CSS styles...\n\nI'm noticing a pattern here—all these empty state classes are doing essentially the same thing with slight variations in padding. I could consolidate them to use the shared `ilu-empty` class and only override padding where the components need something different. The key patterns to extract are the empty state styling and the selection bar pattern that appears across multiple files.\n\nRather than refactoring all 17 files at once, I'll focus on the highest-impact consolidations: the empty states, selection bars, and stats grids. For each pattern, I need to ensure the shared utility exists in panel-utilities.css, remove the duplicate CSS rules from the component files, and update the JSX to use the composed classes.\n\nNow I need to identify which JSX files are actually using these CSS class names so I can update them to reference the shared utilities instead. I see the issue now—the `ilu-empty` base class sets padding to 24px, but some components need different values like 20px or 40px. Rather than removing the component-specific rules entirely, I should keep minimal overrides that just adjust the padding while letting the shared utility handle the rest. This way each file gets the base empty state styling from `ilu-empty` and then applies its own padding adjustment through composition. Now I need to tackle the selection bar pattern.\n\nI'm creating a new utility for the selection bar that handles the flex layout, spacing, background, and border styling. Then I'll identify which component files have their own selection bar classes—EnrichmentQueue, EntityBrowser, and ResultsPanel—and see what customizations each one needs, like sticky positioning or flex wrapping. After that I'll move on to the stats grid pattern.\n\nDefining the stats grid utility... Still writing CSS utilities...\n\nNow I'm shifting to the JSX side—I need to locate the component files and update their className references to use these new utility classes instead of the old component-specific ones. each class is used:\n- cip-empty-state → ChronicleImagePicker.jsx/tsx\n- eps-empty-list → EntryPointStep.jsx/tsx\n- sstep-empty → StyleStep.jsx/tsx\n- eq-empty → EnrichmentQueue.jsx/tsx\n- eb-list-empty → EntityBrowser.jsx/tsx\n- ipm-empty → ImagePickerModal.jsx/tsx\n- rp-empty-state → ResultsPanel.jsx/tsx\n- storage-panel-empty → StoragePanel.jsx/tsx\n- style-editor-empty → StyleLibraryEditor.jsx/tsx\n- tps-empty → TraitPaletteSection.jsx/tsx\n- ers-empty-events → EventResolutionStep.jsx/tsx\n- edv-no-enrichment → EntityDetailView.jsx/tsx\n\nThat's a lot of files to touch. I think the right approach here is to follow the composition pattern from ADR-029: add the shared `ilu-empty` class alongside the component-specific class, then trim the CSS to only keep component-specific overrides. So I'll update the CSS files to remove duplicated declarations and add `ilu-empty` to the JSX className attributes. I'm reviewing the actual padding values across all the component files to determine which ones need to override the base `ilu-empty` class—most use 40px, some use 20px or 16px, so nearly all of them require keeping their component-specific class for the padding override while relying on the shared class for the structural styles like centering and text color.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement this systematically: I'll update the panel utilities CSS first with the new shared pattern, then batch through all the CSS files to remove the duplicated declarations, add the class names to the JSX files, and document the changes. Let me start by finding all the JSX usages with grep so I can update them in parallel. to find them.\n\nHmm, this is getting really involved. Let me focus on the most impactful changes and make those well. I'll extend panel-utilities.css with the shared patterns, use regex to trim the duplicated empty state declarations across all 17 CSS files, and batch-update the JSX with grep-and-replace.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the scope here. The similarity score is moderate at 0.43, and the impact is low since all these files are in the same directory. The most practical approach is to add the four most duplicated patterns to panel-utilities.css, leverage the existing `ilu-empty` class for empty states, and update each CSS file to reference these shared utilities instead.\n\nNow I'm ready to start. I'll begin by extending panel-utilities.css with the selection bar, stats grid, thumb cover, and thumb placeholder patterns, then work through each CSS file to remove the duplicates and update the JSX to compose the shared class names. Still writing styles... I'm noticing that `.bie-heading` is almost identical to `ilu-section-label` except for the font-weight difference, and `.bie-section-label` uses raw RGB color values instead of CSS variables like the rest of the file—there's an inconsistency in how colors are defined across these classes.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture, `BackrefImageEditor.css` is marked as canonical but it's actually the one using hardcoded colors throughout, while the variant files properly use CSS custom properties. This suggests the canonical designation might be incorrect, and the real pattern I should follow is using CSS variables combined with shared utility classes from `panel-utilities.css`.\n\nSo my approach is to establish that as the standard and fix `BackrefImageEditor.css` accordingly.\n\nNow I'm ready to start the actual work: expand `panel-utilities.css` with the missing utility classes, migrate `BackrefImageEditor.css` to use CSS variables, trim the variant files down to just their overrides since the base styles will live in utilities, and update the JSX files to reference the new utility classes. Now I'm ready to start executing. I'll create a todo list and work through panel-utilities.css first, then tackle each file systematically. I'm noticing the BackrefImageEditor has hardcoded colors throughout that need mapping to CSS variables—things like `rgb(255 255 255 / 50%)` which maps to something like `var(--text-muted)` in a dark theme, and `rgb(255 255 255 / 90%)` which is closer to the primary text color.\n\nThe real issue is these are dark-theme-only values, so migrating them to proper CSS custom properties will actually fix the theme drift problem. My plan is to focus on CSS consolidation, update panel-utilities.css with shared patterns, trim duplicates across all 17 CSS files, and then update the JSX files where it'll have the most impact. I see the real issue now—BackrefImageEditor.css is using hardcoded RGB values instead of CSS variables like the rest of the codebase, making it the outlier that needs fixing rather than the canonical source. The actual pattern I should follow is CSS custom properties combined with shared utilities from panel-utilities.css. I'll tackle this systematically: extend panel-utilities.css first, migrate BackrefImageEditor.css to use variables, trim duplicate empty state styles across all 17 files, update the JSX components, and spin up parallel agents to locate all the relevant files while I work through the CSS refactoring.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_i