{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N17berXq2nCndwv7pxbU7W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the layering:\n\n1. **shared-components styles** are imported first via `@the-canonry/shared-components/styles` (in main.jsx and CoherenceEngineRemote.jsx)\n2. **coherence-engine local styles** are imported via `styles/index.css`, which includes:\n   - `compact-overrides.css` — overrides shared component sizing with compact values\n   - `card.css` — full reimplementation of card styles\n   - `editor.css` — full reimplementation of editor styles\n\n**The problem**: Instead of using compact-overrides to override just the properties that differ (like they do for buttons, modals, toggles, sections, forms, and dropdowns), the coherence-engine maintains FULL COPIES of card.css and editor.css with all the compact sizing baked in. This is exactly the drift pattern — the compact overrides approach is already established but wasn't consistently applied to card and editor styles.\n\n\n**The fix**: I need to delete the coherence-engine's local card.css and editor.css files, moving their compact-specific overrides into compact-overrides.css, then remove the corresponding import statements from styles/index.css. For the three component-specific CSS files (dependency-viewer, naming-profile-viewer, validation), those are genuinely unique to each component and don't need consolidation — they follow similar patterns but with different class names and implementations.\n\nActually, rereading the audit, I see the cluster includes both the shared styles and component files. The real consolidation targets are card.css and editor.css, which are duplicated between coherence-engine and shared-components. The component CSS files have 46% similarity because they're using the same design tokens and layout patterns, which is expected and not a problem.\n\nFor those component files, the solution isn't to consolidate them—it's to extract the shared patterns they all follow into reusable utility classes. Things like section styling, headers with flex layouts, count badges, item rows, and empty states appear across all three files but with different class names. I can create a set of shared utility classes that each component CSS file can compose instead of duplicating the pattern logic.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the shared-components folder already has dedicated files for these patterns—card.css, section.css, badge.css, empty-state.css, and item-row.css. So the real issue is that the component CSS files are defining their own prefixed versions of these patterns instead of reusing what's already there. I should check what those shared files actually contain and then update the component files to use them directly.\n\nThe task is clear though: consolidate duplicated styles into shared-components and update the import paths. Let me start with the obvious wins—the coherence-engine card.css and editor.css files have overrides that should move into compact-overrides.css. Then I'll systematically compare the card.css files to see which rules are truly identical versus which are legitimate component-specific customizations. Still writing styles... Continuing with the spacing and sizing adjustments across the remaining card components—the info section gets more left margin, titles and subtitles scale up with adjusted gaps, the body padding increases, and nested cards receive the same dark background and larger radius treatment with consistent spacing throughout. Still writing styles... Continuing through the style differences, I see the coherence-engine variant uses a more compact design with smaller spacing and typography throughout, swaps the nested background color for a darker shade, and appears to handle overflow differently for the condition list.\n\nLooking at the editor.css files now, the coherence-engine version is actually just a stripped-down subset of what's in shared-components—most of the core styling is identical, but it's missing entire sections like the modal styles, button variants, and accordion components that the shared version includes.\n\nMy approach is to consolidate these files by extracting the coherence-engine-specific overrides into compact-overrides.css, then remove the local copies from the coherence-engine imports and delete those files entirely. I'll do the same for card.css first, then tackle editor.css, and finally handle the component-specific CSS files like dependency-viewer and naming-profile-viewer, which follow similar design patterns but need their own customizations.\n\nNow I'm looking at whether these component files should leverage the shared pattern classes directly or keep their own prefixed versions. Let me examine the actual structure of these components to see how much they're duplicating versus what's truly unique to each one. card, border, border-radius — dependency-viewer & validation (naming-profile has it too)\n5. **Section header**: flex, space-between, cursor pointer — dependency-viewer & validation\n6. **Section count**: font-size-sm, bg-dark, pill radius — dependency-viewer & validation\n7. **Expand icon**: font-size-sm, text-muted, transition — dependency-viewer & validation\n8. **Item row**: flex, space-between, bg-dark, border-light — dependency-viewer\n9. **Badge**: font-size-xs, semibold, padding, radius-sm — dependency-viewer & naming-profile\n10. **Empty state**: text-align center, padding-4xl, text-muted — dependency-viewer & naming-profile\n\nThese overlap with existing shared-components patterns, but the components use component-prefixed class names like `.dependency-viewer-section` rather than shared generic ones. The cleanest approach would be creating a shared viewer CSS module and updating the JSX to use those names, but that's a larger refactor. Instead, I could extract the common patterns into a shared viewer-base.css file that the three component CSS files import, keeping only their unique styles locally. I'm identifying the recurring CSS patterns across these component files—container layouts with consistent padding and max-widths, header structures using flexbox for alignment, title styling with typography variables, and card sections with borders and backgrounds. These patterns are consistent enough that I can extract them into a shared viewer utility stylesheet and then have each component reference those base classes rather than duplicating the code. Still writing styles...\n\nActually, I'm noticing that the shared-components library already has some of these patterns defined in existing files like `section.css` and `card.css`, so I should check what's already available before duplicating styles.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture, `card.css` and `editor.css` are clear duplicates between coherence-engine and shared-components that should be consolidated, but the component-specific CSS files use their own class names, which means any consolidation would require changes in both the CSS and JSX. For now, the practical move is to focus on consolidating the card styles first. .css and editor.css (clear win, no JSX changes needed)\n2. Create a shared \"viewer-section\" utility CSS module that provides the common viewer patterns as utility classes\n3. Document that the component CSS files should progressively adopt the shared utility classes\n\nActually, I think for the component CSS files, the most practical consolidation without massive JSX refactoring is:\n- Create a shared CSS module with the common viewer patterns as standalone classes\n- These can be composed alongside the component-specific classes\n- Over time, components can migrate to using only the shared classes\n\nBut actually, let me take a simpler approach. Looking at the three CSS files, they share:\n1. The \"section card\" pattern (bg-card, border, radius-lg)\n2. The \"collapsible section header\" pattern (flex, space-between, cursor-pointer, hover effect)  \n3. The \"count badge\" pattern (font-size-sm, bg-dark, pill)\n4. The \"expand icon\" pattern\n5. The \"empty state\" pattern\n\nThese patterns are already covered by existing shared components like section.css, item-row.css, badge.css, and empty-state.css. The component CSS files are just recreating them with different names, so the real solution would be updating the JSX to use the shared classes instead. Since that's a big refactor, I could create a viewer-patterns.css with composable utilities and document the existing component files as tech debt to migrate later.\n\nBut I'm overcomplicating this. Let me focus on what I can actually consolidate: card.css and editor.css into the coherence-engine compact overrides, and then extract the truly duplicated patterns from the component CSS files into a shared module. Still writing viewer styles... Still writing styles...\n\nI'm realizing that extracting these shared styles into a common file would require updating all the class names across the components, since each one currently uses its own naming convention like `.dependency-viewer-section` instead of the generic `.viewer-section`.\n\nThe task requires actual file edits, so I can't just document this as a follow-up. Let me reconsider the approach — I should create a shared patterns file with the common styles and then systematically update the JSX to use the standardized class names across all three component files. I'm realizing I need to narrow the scope here—the viewer CSS extraction is getting too complicated without JSX changes, so let me focus on the card and editor consolidation as the main deliverables, then handle the viewer files by actually updating their JSX to use shared class names rather than keeping the prefixed ones.\n\nLooking at the CSS cluster analysis, the real wins are eliminating the duplicate card.css and editor.css files in the CE folder since they're nearly identical to the shared-components versions. The three viewer component files have pattern-level 