d Lore use base size, and EraNarrative bumps up to large with tighter line-height. Discovery also adds a deep background color that the others don't have.\n\nI'm seeing the pattern now: the three inline sections share a nearly identical structure, while EraNarrative operates as a modal with its own distinct styling. I'll consolidate the inline sections into a shared CSS foundation with parameterized custom properties, then handle EraNarrative's modal overlay and close button separately.\n\nBuilding the shared CSS file... Adding shared class names to the remaining components — DiscoveryStory gets narrative and overlay styling, LoreSection gets its content classes, and EraNarrative gets the modal overlay and close button classes.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to structure the CSS: each component file should only define custom property values specific to that component, along with any unique layout rules that differ from the base styles, while relying on the shared base classes for common styling.\n\nWriting component CSS files... Still writing component styles... Still writing styles... Still writing component styles... Refining the CSS architecture approach — I realize with custom properties, I can eliminate the component-specific header class entirely. The shared `.archivist-section-hdr` class will use the `--_section-header-bg` variable that each component's root element defines, so I only need the root class setting the custom properties and any truly unique component rules. Let me trace through what the ChainLinkSection CSS actually needs now. Continuing with the text styling—I need to reconcile the ChainLink's small font size with the shared narrative base that uses relaxed line height. The component uses serif font at a smaller size with purple coloring, so I'll need to keep those specifics while leveraging the shared narrative foundation. Looking at the font metrics across all four components, I see that font-size varies everywhere so it shouldn't live in the base, but line-height is consistently `var(--line-height-relaxed)` in ChainLink and Lore, making that a good candidate for the shared styles. Discovery and EraNarrative will need their own overrides since they use different values.\n\nNow I'm setting up the CSS structure with a shared base file that handles the common structural patterns — margins, borders, border-radius, and background — while letting each component define its own typography and color theme through custom properties.\n\nWriting the stylesheet... Still writing styles... Updating the class names to use the shared archivist base styles, then I'll strip out the duplicate header and icon rules from ChainLinkSection.css, keeping only the unique properties like the flow layout, location styling, and content grid that are specific to chain links.\n\nFor EraNarrative, I need to be careful since its modal structure is different — I'll share the overlay and close button styles but keep the modal itself separate. I'm also thinking through how custom properties work with shorthand values like `background` to make sure they'll render correctly across browsers.\n\nNow I need to check the existing eslint configuration to understand how to add the guard rule properly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4414,"cache_read_input_tokens":62949,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4414},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63b294a3-cb88-4d6f-836a-2de3de08695c","uuid":"cd7ba46a-8915-498a-83a5-78e9a127355c"}
