{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MQK97rjv22NdXxTo7ySxAM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So illuminator defines its own CSS variables in `App.css` — `--bg-tertiary: #2d4a6f`. The shared theme doesn't have this variable. This means the shared button.css uses `--color-bg-card: #1e3a5f` while illuminator components use `--bg-tertiary: #2d4a6f` — they're *different colors*.\n\nThis is important. Since these three CSS files all live within the illuminator app, they use illuminator's local CSS variables. If I move styles to the shared-components package, they'd need to use the shared theme variables, which would change the actual colors.\n \nWait, I'm realizing the duplication is actually contained within the illuminator app itself, not spread across multiple apps. So I should create a shared CSS file within illuminator that consolidates the common patterns and uses illuminator's own variables like `--bg-tertiary` and `--border-color`, rather than trying to move this into the shared-components package where the variable names don't match.\n\nLooking at what's actually being duplicated, I'm seeing a \"version toolbar\" pattern that shows up across HistorianEditionComparison, ChronicleVersionSelector, and WorkspaceHeader — version selectors, active state badges, and action buttons all following the same styling approach. Let me think through what the core reusable pieces actually are here.\n\nThe active badge is nearly identical everywhere — a green pill showing \"Active\" — but the existing badge.css has a slightly different green and is missing the pill-shaped border radius. The compact buttons are just smaller versions of the secondary button style. And there's a compact select component used in both version selectors. I think the cleanest solution is to create a dedicated CSS file for these version toolbar elements...\n\nActually, wait — the architecture guidelines say shared patterns should live in a global stylesheet, but these components are illuminator-specific and rely on variables like `--bg-tertiary` that don't exist in the shared theme. So these patterns probably belong in the illuminator app itself rather than trying to force them into shared.\n\nThe practical approach is to create a small utilities file within illuminator that consolidates the toolbar button and active badge styles, then have each component import and use those shared classes instead of duplicating the CSS.\n\nWait, I should check how the illuminator already imports shared styles from the shared-components package to see if I can leverage that existing pattern rather than creating something new.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":73379,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":879},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"012353ca-34db-4cb3-82b9-4cf23efde1ac","uuid":"5ca783de-f4ad-4dc1-844b-43ed08c0af40"}
