{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LAdZ1fQkC8j1qT7m3yp121","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to understand the specifics of the duplication. Let me look at the structural overlap more carefully.\n\nBoth files share the same matrix UI pattern:\n1. **Container** - flex column, 100% height\n2. **Header** - title + subtitle\n3. **Toolbar** - search input with focus style\n4. **Matrix container** - scrollable area with border/radius\n5. **Matrix table** - full-width, collapsed borders, sticky headers\n6. **Table header** - sticky, dark bg, uppercase, small font\n7. **Row styles** - hover background transition\n8. **Cells** - centered text alignment\n\n\n9. **Legend** - flex row, bottom area with bg/border\n\nThe real distinction is that WeightMatrixEditor uses hardcoded pixel values while CoverageMatrix relies on CSS custom properties with fallbacks. CoverageMatrix is already in shared-components and follows a better pattern with variables, but the audit designated WeightMatrixEditor as the canonical version.\n\nNow I'm seeing the actual issue - both files are duplicating the same structural patterns independently. The solution is to extract those shared patterns (matrix container, table, sticky headers, legend, toolbar, search) into a common CSS module in shared-components, then have both components import and extend it with only their specific overrides. I'm realizing the class naming difference is a problem—WeightMatrixEditor and CoverageMatrix use completely different selectors, so I can't just extract shared styles into a base file without either renaming all the classes or using CSS custom properties to make the structure reusable across both components. I extract shared base styles, I'd need to either:\na) Create a shared class namespace and update both JSX files, or\nb) Use a CSS nesting/composition approach\n\nLooking at what other CSS consolidations have done in this project, let me check the existing patterns.\n\nActually, looking at the existing shared styles in shared-components like `panel-utilities.css`, `remote-shell.css`, `viewer.css` — these define utility classes that components import alongside their own styles. Components adopt the shared class names directly.\n\nThe best approach for a matrix pattern would be:\n1. Create `packages/shared-components/src/styles/components/matrix-base.css` with the shared structural patterns using generic class names (e.g., `.matrix-container`, `.matrix-table`, `.matrix-row`, `.matrix-legend`, `.matrix-toolbar`, `.matrix-search`)\n2. Update WeightMatrixEditor to import this base and remove redundant rules\n3. Update CoverageMatrix to use these class names and import the base, removing redundant rules\n\nBut this requires changing CoverageMatrix's JSX class names from `cm-*` to `matrix-*` which is a JSX change too. Let me look at the components themselves to assess scope.\n\nBoth are single-use components with only 40% similarity, so forcing class name alignment would mean editing JSX files for minimal benefit. A more pragmatic approach: extract only the truly identical structural rules into a shared base CSS file with generic class names like `.data-matrix`, `.data-matrix-table`, `.data-matrix-row`, then update both CSS and JSX files to reference these shared patterns.\n\nI should check how other CSS consolidations in this project handle this — looking at existing shared CSS patterns to understand the convention.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8600,"cache_read_input_tokens":61679,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8600},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e25f0ac-8e93-4dfd-8fc9-5da30702dc9f","uuid":"8b59efd2-57d1-497e-9e8e-43185576612e"}
