{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011gnNx29QYs8vgVYDMG3rvN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the situation. Both files live in the same directory (`packages/shared-components/src/styles/components/`) and are imported from the shared styles index. Let me understand the drift:\n\n1. **empty-state.css** (canonical): Centered layout pattern for \"nothing here\" states\n2. **error-boundary.css** (variant): Centered layout pattern for error fallback display\n\nThe 43% similarity comes from their shared structural pattern:\n- Both use `display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center;`\n- Both have a title with `font-size: var(--font-size-2xl); font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);`\n\n\n- Both have a description/message with `color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 400px;`\n\nThe key difference is that error-boundary adds height constraints, a styled icon with danger colors and a circular background, plus a retry button. So the real opportunity here is extracting that shared centered layout foundation into empty-state.css and having error-boundary.css build on top of it rather than duplicate the pattern. Now I need to check which components actually consume these CSS classes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5203,"cache_read_input_tokens":52933,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5203},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a207061-1c30-40ba-a375-5d0443f621d8","uuid":"1d15d0f9-6e41-4bbf-aada-201efc17d4fb"}
