on] - Emoji icon to display\n * @param {string} props.title - Title text\n * @param {string} [props.description] - Description text\n * @param {React.ReactNode} [props.children] - Additional content (e.g., action button)\n * @param {string} [props.className] - Optional additional class names\n */\nexport function EmptyState({ icon, title, description, children, className = '' }) {\n  return (\n    <div className={`empty-state ${className}`.trim()}>\n      {icon && <div className=\"empty-state-icon\">{icon}</div>}\n      <div className=\"empty-state-title\">{title}</div>\n      {description && <div className=\"empty-state-desc\">{description}</div>}\n      {children}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default EmptyState;\n\nEmptyState.propTypes = {\n  icon: PropTypes.string,\n  title: PropTypes.string.isRequired,\n  description: PropTypes.string,\n  children: PropTypes.node,\n  className: PropTypes.string,\n};\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MAzZFvXpkFw3L5ccBaYvWZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. The consolidation strategy:\n\n1. **empty-state.css** already defines the base centered-message layout pattern\n2. **error-boundary.css** duplicates that base layout then adds error-specific styling\n3. The ErrorBoundary component should compose by using `empty-state` as a base class alongside `error-boundary` for overrides\n\nHere's the plan:\n1. Refactor `error-boundary.css` to ONLY contain the error-boundary-specific overrides, with the `error-boundary` class composing on top of `empty-state`\n2. Update `ErrorBoundary.jsx` to use both class names: `empty-state error-boundary`\n\n\n3. For the child elements, I'll apply the same composition pattern—the title and message elements will inherit from their empty-state counterparts while error-boundary-specific classes handle any visual differences, and the icon stays unique to the error boundary.\n\nNow I'm comparing the actual CSS properties between the two components to see where they overlap and where they diverge.\n\nThe containers are nearly identical except the error boundary adds a minimum height constraint. For the titles, they're exactly the same. The descriptions differ slightly—the error boundary uses a smaller font size and adds word-break handling. The icons are completely different, with the error boundary using a circular danger badge instead of a simple icon.\n\nMy plan is to reuse the empty-state styles as the foundation and layer error-boundary-specific overrides on top.\n\nWriting error-boundary styles... Still writing styles...\n\nI'm realizing the error-boundary-message class needs to override the font-size from empty-state-desc since they use different sizes, and it also adds word-break handling for better text wrapping in error scenarios. Updating error-boundary.css to remove all the duplicated styling — I'll strip out the flex layout, padding, and color properties from the main container since those come from empty-state.css now, and delete the title rule entirely since it was identical. The message class will only keep the font-size and word-break overrides, while the icon and retry button styles stay untouched since they're unique to the error boundary.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1229,"cache_read_input_tokens":61351,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1229},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a207061-1c30-40ba-a375-5d0443f621d8","uuid":"311b157f-fc5a-4773-8048-6cdb2b921798"}
