{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AuCDU8BhnviCyqNaJ3RvPU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me understand the situation:\n\n1. **Canonical pattern**: lore-weave and name-forge (library roots, NOT webui) have vitest configured with `vitest.config.ts` files and vitest devDependencies. They have test scripts but currently point to \"no tests to run\" messages.\n\n2. **Variants to migrate**: 7 webui apps have NO test infrastructure — no vitest config, no test scripts, no test dependencies.\n\n3. **But**: Even the canonical apps' webui directories (lore-weave/webui, name-forge/webui) have no test infrastructure either. \n\nThe vitest config only exists in the library root directories, so I need to set up vitest across all webui apps with jsdom environment for React testing, along with @testing-library/react and appropriate test scripts in their package.json files. The goal is standardizing on the `vitest-configured-no-tests` pattern — having the infrastructure in place without necessarily having test files yet. infrastructure, not writing tests.\n\nLet me think about what a proper webui vitest config looks like. The webui apps are React apps (using vite + @vitejs/plugin-react), so they need:\n- `environment: 'jsdom'` (not 'node' like the lib configs)\n- `@testing-library/react` and `@testing-library/jest-dom` as devDependencies for React component testing\n- `vitest` and `@vitest/coverage-v8` as devDependencies\n- A vitest.config.ts file\n- Test scripts in package.json\n\nActually, wait. The canonical pattern is specifically `vitest-configured-no-tests`. The existing configs in lore-weave and name-forge are for Node/library code. For webui apps, we need a React-appropriate vitest config.\n\nSo the actual work here is setting up vitest across all 9 webui apps by adding config files, test dependencies, and npm scripts. The recommendation from the work file is to establish a standard pattern with @testing-library/react and co-located test files next to components. I need to create a unified vitest configuration that works across all 7 webui apps regardless of whether they use TypeScript or JavaScript, then add the necessary dependencies like vitest, coverage tools, and React testing libraries to each app's package.json.\n\nWriting vitest config... I'm realizing the canonical apps use placeholder test scripts because their tests were archived, but for these new variant apps I should set up real test infrastructure from the start—so vitest commands that will actually run tests once they're written, rather than noop placeholders.\n\nFor dependencies, I need to balance the recommendation to include `@testing-library/react` with the fact that there are no tests yet. I'll add vitest, the coverage provider, jsdom for React testing, and the testing library packages since they're part of the standard webui setup—developers will need them when they start writing tests anyway.\n\nI should just edit the package.json files directly rather than running install commands, since that avoids any potential side effects.\n\nNow I need to check whether the webui apps already have vite.config files that I should extend, or if I should create separate vitest.config.ts files — the canonical pattern suggests keeping them separate. From my earlier audit, I confirmed that lore-weave and name-forge webui directories don't have test scripts or vitest dependencies yet, so I'll be starting from scratch with those.\n\nSince the migration work focuses on the 7 explicitly listed apps, I'll prioritize those and note the other two as potential future targets. Let me examine what the existing vite.config files look like across the webui apps to see if they're using TypeScript or JavaScript.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3153,"cache_read_input_tokens":58900,"cache_creation":{"ephe