{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FjjnUxVg68pi1icga5UwJC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the ESLint config setup. I need to:\n\n1. Create a custom ESLint rule that warns on `.js`/`.jsx` files (enforcing TypeScript for new code)\n2. Create a rule that bans `import PropTypes from 'prop-types'` (preventing regression in shared-components)\n\nFor the TypeScript enforcement, I have two options:\na. A file-level rule that reports on the file itself if its extension is .js/.jsx\nb. A `no-restricted-imports` rule banning `prop-types`\n\nBoth make sense. Let me create:\n\n1. `no-new-js-files.js` — Custom rule that reports when a .js/.jsx file is encountered in directories that should be TypeScript\n\n\n2. Ban `prop-types` import via `no-restricted-imports` (simpler, built-in)\n\nSince ESLint runs on files matching the glob pattern, checking the filename extension directly in a custom rule makes sense. The rule should detect .js/.jsx files and suggest converting them to .ts/.tsx, while excluding certain directories like vite config files that intentionally stay as JavaScript.\n\nFor the PropTypes issue, I'll use the built-in `no-restricted-imports` rule to ban the `prop-types` package directly. For enforcing TypeScript file extensions in source directories, I can create a custom rule that checks the filename on the `Program` node—this is a valid pattern teams use. I'll set up both the custom rule and the `no-restricted-imports` configuration, though I notice the ESLint config already has some overrides for .js/.jsx files that I should account for. files. So JS files are supported but with reduced checking. The goal of the guard is to ensure NEW files are TypeScript, not to break existing JS files.\n\nLet me think about what enforceable constraints I can create:\n\n1. **Ban `prop-types` import** — This prevents anyone from adding PropTypes to new or existing files. Since we just converted shared-components to TypeScript interfaces, importing PropTypes is the old pattern.\n\n2. **Warn on JS/JSX file creation** — A custom rule that checks the filename extension. This is the most direct way to enforce \"new files must be .ts/.tsx\".\n\n3. **Ban PropTypes.* usage** — Even if someone doesn't import it, they might use it from a local variable.\n\nLet me implement all three constraints. The `no-restricted-imports` for prop-types is free (built-in). The custom rule for file extension is simple. Now I'll write the custom rule and update the config.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4115,"cache_read_input_tokens":105477,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4115},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e54c7e2e-0d44-4856-a582-afde6e85abbc","uuid":"8c20c846-342c-455d-a918-8c76bb568996"}
